Considerations
	•	Performance: Using pre-computed embeddings eliminates the need to call the EmbeddingModel for each query, which can significantly improve performance, especially for frequent searches.
	•	Dimensionality Match: Ensure the numDimensions in your index matches the length of your pre-computed embedding vectors to avoid errors.
	•	Index Maintenance: If your pre-computed embeddings are updated, you’ll need to re-index the collection accordingly.
	•	Spring AI Limitation: The current Spring AI API does not provide a direct way to pass a pre-computed vector to similaritySearch. This workaround relies on the native MongoDB client, which might not align perfectly with Spring AI’s abstraction layer. Future versions of Spring AI might address this with additional configuration options.
Alternative Approach
If you prefer to stay within Spring AI’s API, you could extend MongoDBAtlasVectorStore to override the similaritySearch behavior. However, this would require custom implementation and might not be officially supported. A simpler approach is to stick with the native client method described above.
Conclusion
By using the native MongoDB client and the $vectorSearch aggregation stage with your pre-computed embedding, you can avoid redundant embedding computations. This approach leverages MongoDB’s vector search capabilities directly and integrates with your existing Spring AI setup. Test this solution with your data to ensure compatibility with your index configuration and application requirements.
